   "text": {
    "L1": "In this play, saying the right thing means being kind and polite to a guest.",
    "L2": "Raju repeats a private rude remark, so the guest feels awkward and Mother feels embarrassed.",
    "L3": "The words in brackets are stage directions. Actors act them, but do not say them aloud.",
    "L4": "Mother is shown turning red, which tells us that she is embarrassed.",
    "L5": "A direct command can sound rude, so soften it with could or would.",
    "L6": "Say, “Could you pass the water, please?” instead of “Give me water.”",
    "L7": "“Would you like some tea?” is a polite offer to a guest.",
    "L8": "Some idioms are not literal: “a piece of cake” means something is very easy."
   },
